<div class="page">
  <div style="display: flex; gap: 50px;">
    <div style="flex: 1;">
      <h2 class="section-heading">Overview</h2>
      <p>The EUCLID DILT (PhD in International Law and Treaty Law) is a distance-delivered, non-resident degree program offered by EUCLID (Euclid University), an intergovernmental treaty-based organization with a global university mandate.</p>
      <p>Its goal is to prepare both our government-sponsored officials and general-public graduate students for high-level positions in the vast realm of applied international law. Positions are typically obtained with relevant international organizations, intergovernmental and non-governmental organizations, as well as multinational corporations and global legal services firms.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="flex: 1;">
      <h2 class="section-heading">Admission Requirements</h2>
      <p>Applicants must have a Master's degree or its international equivalent, issued by an institution recognized by the appropriate public agency.</p>
      <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
        <li class="list-item">Government-sponsored applicants (who are already government staff) may be admitted without a qualifying degree if their level of education and experience is deemed sufficient.</li>
        <li class="list-item">EUCLID may require these participants to complete bridge courses prior to enrolling in the DILT.</li>
        <li class="list-item">Fluency in English (spoken and written) is required.</li>
        <li class="list-item">Students are expected to be in a position to dedicate 10 to 15 effective hours of study on a weekly basis.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="display: flex; gap: 50px; margin-top: 40px;">
    <div style="flex: 1;">
      <h2 class="section-heading">Program Objectives</h2>
      <p>The DILT offers an in-depth examination of the vast field of international relations, including international law and treaty law, intergovernmental organizations, complex negotiations, as well as pressing global issues. Its capstone is a doctoral dissertation which will establish the candidate as a subject-matter specialist in a specialized topic of relevance.</p>
      <p>This PhD program focuses on the mastery of relevant areas of international law, with an initial focus on the law of treaties (how treaties are made), followed by a systematic studies of concrete applications of treaty law to practical domains such as international litigation, international property or maritime law. Rather than focusing on one area in particular (this is not a specialized degree), the EUCLID DILT provides a broad and deep overview of the most indispensable regimes.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="flex: 1;">
      <h2 class="section-heading">Methodology</h2>
      <p>This program is offered on an asynchronous and open-enrollment basis with occasional synchronous in-class support.</p>
      <p>The DILT is personalized for every student and consists in a sequence of courses (academic modules) which are delivered using the following methods / media formats:</p>
      <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
        <li class="list-item">Standard textbooks</li>
        <li class="list-item">DVD lectures / online videos</li>
        <li class="list-item">MP3 podcasts and webcasts</li>
        <li class="list-item">Internal EUCLID course packs</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="display: flex; gap: 50px;">
    <div style="flex: 1;">
      <h2 class="section-heading">Program Courses</h2>
      <p>EUCLID DILT Course Sequence:</p>
      <p>(including optional courses marked with *):</p>
      <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
        <li class="list-item">International Law and Treaty Law (INT-LAW) (4 UCH / 6 ECTS)</li>
        <li class="list-item">Argumentation and Critical Thinking (ARG-CT) (3 UCH / 3 ECTS)</li>
        <li class="list-item">Diplomacy: Theory and Practice (DIP-401) (4 UCH / 6 ECTS)</li>
        <li class="list-item">International Law and Treaty Law (INT-LAW) (5 UCH / 7.5 ECTS)</li>
        <li class="list-item">International Human Rights Law (IHR-HR) (3 UCH / 4.5 ECTS)</li>
        <li class="list-item">European Law (EUR-510) (3 UCH / 4.5 ECTS)</li>
        <li class="list-item">Private International Law (PIL-401) (4 UCH / 6 ECTS)</li>
        <li class="list-item">International IP Law (INT-IP) (3 UCH / 3 ECTS)</li>
        <li class="list-item">International Criminal Law (ICL-IC) (3 UCH / 3 ECTS)</li>
        <li class="list-item">Maritime Law / LOS (LMM-LOS) (4 UCH / 6 ECTS)</li>
        <li class="list-item">International Taxation Issues (LLM-TAX) (4 UCH / 6 ECTS)</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div style="flex: 1;">
      <h2 class="section-heading">Course Format</h2>
      <p>Typically, a course consists of 7 periods which logically organize and sequence the assigned materials that are studied on an individual basis. Courses assignments include (1) response papers after each study period (2) the composition of academic papers based on the text-books, (3) the creation of a quiz which demonstrates the student's grasp of the subject-matter scope and particulars.</p>
      <p>Students are often connected in "pairs" or "trios" which allows them to interact and exchange on their coursework while moving along in the program at their own pace.</p>
      <p>Each course is capped by an extensive 45-minute long oral examination (typically done using Skype) which allows the supervising instructor to enter the composite final grade.</p>
      <p>Overall, this program emphasizes the ability to understand systemic interactions as well as to express logical, respectful and persuasive arguments, which are the hallmarks of a successful professional. At the same time, respect for the diversity of opinions, cultural backgrounds and values is emphasized.</p>
      <p>The ability to write professional, journal-grade papers is one of the essential objectives of this program. All students must complete the program having acquired and demonstrated graduate-level expertise in a specific aspect of diplomacy and international affairs that will be of lasting interest to academia, government or the private sector.</p>
      <p>The curriculum also calls for the ability to present oral reports or lectures (webcasts), delivered to the course tutor in an acceptable digital format.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="display: flex; gap: 50px;">
    <div style="flex: 1;">
      <h2 class="section-heading">Accreditation and Recognition</h2>
      <p>EUCLID (Pôle Universitaire Euclid) / Euclid University is an intergovernmental organization with a university charter established by multilateral treaty in 2008. The treaty itself is registered and published in full by the United Nations Treaty Section with certificate reference I-49006/49007 (visit <span data-field="treaty_link">{{ treaty_link }}</span>).</p>
      <p>As indicated by a United Nations Letter (0218/RA/CM/ONU/12), EUCLID (and the UN-related institutions including the United Nations University, the European University Institute, the African Union, the African Union, and UNESCO) are recognized by the United Nations and UNESCO as being accredited.</p>
      <p>Having the status of intergovernmental non-profit institution under public international law (like NATO or the World Bank), EUCLID is one of the few universities that enjoying international law accreditation or validation by multiple governments spanning 3 continents (Africa, Asia, Pacific). EUCLID has full diplomatic status in its headquarters and is recognized by the Government of Foreign Affairs, as well as by the United Nations, as a treaty-based organization.</p>
      <p>EUCLID's institutional and program accreditation is also confirmed by agreements signed with other intergovernmental organizations (ICI, ECOWAS, CAFRAD).</p>
      <p>This graduate program specifically follows in international guidelines for a master's degree, and it is fully documented to facilitate international transfers. A standard EU 'Diploma Supplement' is available upon request after graduation.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="flex: 1;">
      <h2 class="section-heading">Research Areas:</h2>
      <p>The thesis research topic is clarified at an early stage, ideally prior to enrollment, and must be approved by the supervisory faculty member.</p>
      <h2 class="section-heading">Peers and Alumni</h2>
      <p>Because EUCLID was established to train active diplomats and international law experts, our students can be assured that their peers and fellow alumni will form a high-level network.</p>
      <h2 class="section-heading">Credit Values</h2>
      <p>Credit values are expressed both in terms of US credit hours (US credit hour) and ECTS (European Credit Transfer System). Typically, 14 hours of classroom instruction over the course of 1 semester represents 1 USCH or 1.5 ECTS (plus self-study time), which is converted to the equivalent amount of study in the context of a distance program.</p>
      <p>The total credit value for the completed DILT, according to international standards, is 90.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="display: flex; gap: 50px;">
    <div style="flex: 1;">
      <h2 class="section-heading">Language Requirements</h2>
      <p>All students must be fluent in English and should be conversant in French, Spanish, Arabic, Chinese or another major world language.</p>
      <h2 class="section-heading">How to apply</h2>
      <p>All interested students must apply online at <span data-field="application_url">{{ application_url }}</span>.</p>
      <p>Government-sponsored applicants may apply online in the same manner as the general public, but they should also contact their National Point of Contact to secure formal endorsement.</p>
      <p>The EUCLID Admissions Officer will send to all applicants an updated version of the "Admissions Checklist" which explains the required documentation to provide and the steps to be followed.</p>
      <p>It must be emphasized that completing a distance-study program at EUCLID requires dedication and persistence. This same trait is expected in applicants who should not expect to be pursued by the Admissions team but rather actively pursue enrollment at EUCLID.</p>
      <h2 class="section-heading">Summary and Outcome</h2>
      <p>It is fair to say that EUCLID's programs are better described as "distance" rather than "online" study programs. This means that students do not have to connect at a particular day and time to participate in synchronous sessions, as is the case with online programs.</p>
      <p>Rather, our programs emphasize the ability to absorb a vast amount of material on a personal basis, which makes them suitable for fast readers who on their own have a record of intense book reading and passionate interest in their field of study.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="flex: 1;">
      <div class="image-container">
        <img src="placeholder_image_4.png" alt="Student studying" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto;">
      </div>
      <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
        <p>EUCLID evaluates the applicant's interpersonal and teamwork skills—which are essential for per-sonal and professional success—on the basis of prior experience confirmed by means of an ex-tensive personal interview. Because EUCLID was established to serve active diplomats and senior civil servants, it is expected that these profes-sionals will have acquired and demon-strated in the course of their careers a high level of expertise.</p>
        <p>In some cases, EUCLID may propose specific courses (which may not be listed as elec-tives) in order to bring inter-personal skills (including public speaking) to executive level.</p>
        <p>Upon completion of the program, every student is expected to have become a subject-matter ex-pert in a global issues.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

